  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 8,
    "lesson_title": "Harmonics of a Fundamental Frequency",
    "lesson_core_move": "Classify sinusoidal components in a periodic continuous-time signal as harmonics by expressing each angular frequency as an integer multiple of the fundamental angular frequency.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Harmonics of a Fundamental Frequency",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Harmonics of a Fundamental Frequency",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In a periodic continuous-time signal, the harmonic test is based on the fundamental angular frequency $\\omega_0$. A sinusoidal component is a harmonic when its angular frequency fits the same spacing pattern as the fundamental frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_n = n\\omega_0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Write the harmonic family as $\\omega_n = n\\omega_0$, where $n$ is a positive integer. The harmonic number is the integer $n$, not the amplitude, the phase, or the sign of the sinusoid.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "n = \\frac{\\omega_n}{\\omega_0}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The simplest case is $\\omega = \\omega_0$, which gives $\\omega/\\omega_0 = 1$ and makes the component the first harmonic. The next integer multiples, such as $2\\omega_0$ and $3\\omega_0$, give the second and third harmonics by the same test.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_1=\\omega_0,\\; \\omega_2=2\\omega_0,\\; \\omega_3=3\\omega_0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the rule is simple: compute $\\omega/\\omega_0$. If the result is a positive integer, the component is a harmonic; if it is not, the component is not a harmonic. In the next examples, you will apply this integer-multiple test to single terms, numeric ratios, nonharmonic ratios, and multiple components.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Harmonics of a Fundamental Frequency",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Identifying Integer-Multiple Angular Frequencies",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For the periodic signal $x(t)=5\\cos(4\\omega_0 t-\\pi/3)$, identify the angular frequency and state which harmonic the sinusoid is.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the coefficient of $t$ inside the sinusoid. The amplitude $5$ and the phase $-\\pi/3$ do not affect the harmonic label, so the only part to read is the angular-frequency factor multiplying $t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega = 4\\omega_0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "From the coefficient on $t$, the angular frequency is $4\\omega_0$. To classify the component, divide by the fundamental angular frequency and check whether the ratio is a positive integer.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{\\omega}{\\omega_0}=\\frac{4\\omega_0}{\\omega_0}=4",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because the ratio is the positive integer $4$, the sinusoid is the fourth harmonic. The amplitude and phase are just clutter; they do not change the harmonic number.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Harmonics of a Fundamental Frequency",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Finding the Harmonic Number of a Sinusoidal Component",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A periodic signal has fundamental angular frequency $\\omega_0=2\\pi$ rad/s and contains the component $4\\cos(10\\pi t+\\pi/6)$. Determine the harmonic number of the component.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the coefficient of $t$ inside the sinusoid. The amplitude $4$ and the phase $\\pi/6$ do not affect the harmonic number, so the only job is to compare the component angular frequency to $\\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega = 10\\pi",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here the fundamental angular frequency is $\\omega_0=2\\pi$. Form the ratio $\\omega/\\omega_0$ and simplify it before naming the harmonic.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{\\omega}{\\omega_0}=\\frac{10\\pi}{2\\pi}=5",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because the ratio is the positive integer $5$, the component is the fifth harmonic. The amplitude and phase are just clutter; they do not change the harmonic number.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Harmonics of a Fundamental Frequency",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Recognizing Frequencies That Are Not Harmonics",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A periodic signal has fundamental angular frequency $\\omega_0=4\\pi$ rad/s and contains the component $3\\sin(10\\pi t-\\pi/4)$. Decide whether the sinusoid is a harmonic.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the coefficient of $t$ inside the sinusoid. The amplitude $3$ and the phase $-\\pi/4$ do not affect the classification, so the only job is to compare the component angular frequency to $\\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega = 10\\pi,\\qquad \\omega_0 = 4\\pi",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Form the ratio $\\omega/\\omega_0$ and simplify it. The ratio can be a fraction only if the component is not a harmonic, because the harmonic test requires a positive integer.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{\\omega}{\\omega_0}=\\frac{10\\pi}{4\\pi}=\\frac{5}{2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because $5/2$ is not a positive integer, the component is not a harmonic. Even though the ratio is a neat fraction, it does not satisfy the integer-multiple test, and the amplitude and phase still do not matter.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Harmonics of a Fundamental Frequency",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Classifying Multiple Sinusoidal Components by Harmonic Number",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(t)=3\\cos(2\\pi t-\\pi/5)+\\sin(6\\pi t)+4\\cos(7\\pi t)$ with fundamental angular frequency $2\\pi$ rad/s, classify each sinusoidal component by harmonic number or as not a harmonic.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Scan the terms one at a time. The amplitude and phase do not affect harmonic status; only the angular frequency in front of $t$ matters.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=3\\cos(2\\pi t-\\pi/5)+\\sin(6\\pi t)+4\\cos(7\\pi t),\\qquad \\omega_0=2\\pi",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read off each angular frequency and divide by $\\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{2\\pi}{2\\pi}=1,\\qquad \\frac{6\\pi}{2\\pi}=3,\\qquad \\frac{7\\pi}{2\\pi}=\\frac{7}{2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So $3\\cos(2\\pi t-\\pi/5)$ is the first harmonic, $\\sin(6\\pi t)$ is the third harmonic, and $4\\cos(7\\pi t)$ is not a harmonic because $7/2$ is not a positive integer. The classification is term-by-term, not based on amplitude or phase.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
